| **i102b** | i102 SHA-256 speedups — Opt-1: short-distance rotations (whole-byte `sha_rotr8`/`sha_rotl8` + a minimal bit residual + `sha_shr8`; deleted the bit-at-a-time `sha_rotr_word_by`); Opt-5: dropped the redundant push/pop in `sha_add4` (callers already reload HL/DE). Documented opcodes only; NIST vectors byte-exact. | DONE — ✅ PRs #318 (−27.4%) + #322 (−2.7%); SHA-256 ~29.4% below baseline (6.45M→4.55M T) | `src/netboot/sha256.asm`; i102 |
| **i102c** | i102 quarter-square 8×8 multiply — replaced the `mul8` shift-add loop with the quarter-square identity `a·b = QSQ[a+b] − QSQ[\|a−b\|]` (`QSQ[n]=⌊n²/4⌋`, a 1022-byte RAM table built by `qsq_init` via a pure-add recurrence). Opt-2: x25519; Opt-3: poly1305 (identical loop). Exhaustive `TestMul8Exhaustive`/`TestPoly1305Mul8Exhaustive` (all 65536 pairs) + RFC/e2e byte-exact. | DONE — ✅ PRs #319 (x25519 −23.0%) + #320 (poly1305 −9.5% / aead −11.5%); later DRY-extracted to `qsq.asm` | `src/netboot/qsq.asm`; `src/netboot/x25519.asm`; `src/netboot/poly1305.asm`; i102 |
| **i102d** | i102 chacha20 redundant push/pop — `cc_add`/`cc_xor` preserved HL/DE that every `cc_qr` caller reloads; dropping the push/pop also let `cc_addstate`'s manual word-advance go (faster AND smaller). RFC 8439 + aead byte-exact. | DONE — ✅ PR #321 (−7.5% chacha20_block; first i102 increment to shrink code) | `src/netboot/chacha20.asm`; i102 |
| **i102e** | i102 x25519 structural opts — Opt-6: dedicated symmetric squaring `fe_sqr` (496 off-diagonal products doubled + 32 diagonal terms, ~half a multiply); Opt-7: constant-aware `fe_mul121665` (96 vs 1024 mul8 for the 3-byte a24); Opt-8: hold the schoolbook inner-loop pointers in IX/IY across `mul8`. Field pins + RFC 7748 + Go-curve25519 e2e byte-exact. | DONE — ✅ PRs #343 (−18.7%) + #345 (−8.6%) + #346 (−5.64%, −45 B); x25519 cumulative 1.604G→1.125G = −29.9% | `src/netboot/x25519.asm`; i102 |
| **i102f** | i102 x25519 Karatsuba field multiply — `fe_schoolbook` (the 32×32→64-byte product behind every `fe_mul`) replaced its flat 1024-`mul8` schoolbook with one level of Karatsuba: split each operand at 16 bytes, three 16×16 sub-multiplies (`kmul16`) for z0/z2/middle (768 `mul8`), the middle coefficient via the subtractive identity `z1 = z0+z2 − (a_lo−a_hi)(b_lo−b_hi)` over absolute differences with a tracked sign bit, then a multi-byte combine (`kadd`/`ksub`). `fe_sqr` left untouched (its symmetric schoolbook already matches Karatsuba's `mul8` count). New `TestFEMulRandom` (1024 random asymmetric pairs) + the existing field pins + RFC 7748 + Go-curve25519 e2e all byte-exact. | DONE — ✅ PR #354 (x25519 −13.5%); x25519 cumulative 1.604G→0.973G = −39.3% | `src/netboot/x25519.asm`; `tools/netboot-oracle/z80/x25519_field_test.go`; i102 |
| **i102g** | i102 `mul8` micro-opts (the 8×8→16 multiply, ~1.66M calls per x25519, shared by x25519 + poly1305) — (1) drop `mul8`'s internal `push de`/`pop hl`: the saved operands `B`/`C` are dead once the difference is taken, so the second `QSQ[\|a−b\|]` lookup reuses `BC` for the table base+entry while `QSQ[a+b]` stays in `DE`; (2) remove the dead `ld d, 0` before every `mul8` call across the x25519 + poly1305 byte-radix loops (`mul8` reads only A and E). `TestMul8Exhaustive` + `TestPoly1305Mul8Exhaustive` (all 65536 pairs each) + RFC 7748 / RFC 8439 / Go e2e byte-exact. | DONE — ✅ PR #355 (x25519 −4.0%, 973.3M→934.1M; also speeds poly1305/aead); x25519 cumulative 1.604G→0.934G = −41.8% | `src/netboot/qsq.asm`; `src/netboot/x25519.asm`; `src/netboot/poly1305.asm`; i102 |
| **i102h** | i102 chacha20 register-based rotates — `cc_rotl16`/`cc_rotl8` (the quarter-round's byte-permute rotates, each invoked ~80× per block: ROTL16 once + ROTL8 thrice via ROTL8/ROTL12/ROTL7) read the 4 word bytes into registers A/B/C/D and write them back permuted in place, instead of bouncing them through the `cc_tmp4` memory temp via `cc_load_tmp` (now deleted — faster **and** smaller). RFC 8439 KAT + block/encrypt + AEAD + TLS-client composite byte-exact. | DONE — ✅ PR #356 (chacha20_block −15.5%, 345.5K→292.0K; chacha20_encrypt −15.3%; also speeds aead) | `src/netboot/chacha20.asm`; i102 |
| **i102i** | i102 x25519 schoolbook carry in a register — the multiply/square inner loops (`kmul16`, the Karatsuba 16×16; `fe_sqr` off-diagonal — together ~88% of x25519's `mul8` calls) kept the running byte carry in memory (`fe_car`: a read + a write per iteration). The loops already `push bc`/`pop bc` around `mul8` to preserve the `djnz` counter `B`; riding the carry in `C` through that same push/pop drops both memory accesses for free, and `dest[i+j] + carry` is folded into one `add a,(iy+0)` (the 9-bit sum added to the product via `DE`). math/big field pins + `TestFEMulRandom` + `TestFESqr*` + RFC 7748 + Go-curve25519 e2e byte-exact. | DONE — ✅ PR #357 (x25519 −6.6%, 934.1M→872.4M) | `src/netboot/x25519.asm`; i102 |
| **i102j** | i102 `mul8` page-aligned table index — `qsq_table` is page-aligned (`&C000`/`&FB00`, low byte 0) and the lookup index `2*(a+b)` / `2*\|a−b\|` is ≤ 1020, so each entry address is formed by adding only the high byte (`ld a,qsq_table>>8; add a,h; ld h,a`) instead of a full `ld de,qsq_table; add hl,de` — the index's low byte is already the address low byte. Saves ~12 T per `mul8` call (~1.77M calls per x25519). Invariant documented at the `qsq_table` equ and guarded by the exhaustive `TestMul8Exhaustive`/`TestPoly1305Mul8Exhaustive` (all 65536 pairs) — any non-page-aligned base fails CI. | DONE — ✅ PR #358 (x25519 −2.7%, 872.4M→848.7M; also speeds poly1305) | `src/netboot/qsq.asm`; i102 |
| **i102k** | i102 reduction/constant-multiply carry in a register — applied the i102i carry-in-`C` pattern to the two remaining byte-radix multiply loops: `fe_mul38_high` (the `×38` high-half fold, run on **every** fe_mul **and** fe_sqr reduction) and `fe_121665_inner` (the `×a24` curve-constant multiply). Both kept the running carry in `fe_car` memory; now it rides the existing `push bc`/`pop bc` around `mul8` in `C`, and the addend is folded into one `add` (`fe_m38` has only the carry; `fe_121665` folds dest+carry via `add a,(iy+0)` like `kmul16`). Completes the carry-register conversion across all byte-radix multiply loops, so the `fe_car` scratch byte is now fully dead and is removed (its last users dropped it in i102i/this PR). Field pins + `TestFEMul121665` + RFC 7748 + Go-curve25519 e2e byte-exact. | DONE — ✅ PR #359 (x25519 −0.5%, 848.7M→844.5M) | `src/netboot/x25519.asm`; i102 |
| **i102l** | i102 `mul8` inlined at the two hottest x25519 sites — the quarter-square `mul8` body (the 8×8→16 multiply, ~1.8M calls per x25519) was a called subroutine paying `call`+`ret` (27 T) per invocation. Refactored into a pyz80 `mul8_body` MACRO (single source-of-truth; the i102c qsq DRY extraction is preserved — `mul8` is now `mul8_body` + `ret`) and expanded INLINE at `kmul16_inner` (the Karatsuba 16×16) and `fe_sqr_inner` (the off-diagonal squarer), ~96% of the calls. The branch over `neg` uses `jr nc,$+4` (skip the 2-byte `neg`) so the macro carries no internal label and expands any number of times. `fe_sqr_diag` + poly1305 + the host field tests keep calling the subroutine. `TestMul8Exhaustive` (all 65536 pairs) + RFC 7748 + Go-curve25519 e2e + the TLS-client composite headroom assertion (`tls_client_end < qsq_table`, ~1.1 KB clearance after ~70 B growth) byte-exact. | DONE — ✅ PR #363 (x25519 −5.77%, 844.5M→795.8M) | `src/netboot/qsq.asm`; `src/netboot/x25519.asm`; i102 |
| **i102n** | i102 **sha256 register-oriented rewrite — increment 1** (resolves q21: do the rewrite, keep cryptographic SHA-256). Replaced `sha256_compress` (memory-to-memory, ~2.5–3k `call`/`ret` per block) with a register-resident design: inline `add4`/`copy4` macros; the σ/Σ rotates held in B/C/D/E (8T `rr r` + free whole-byte relabels, not 15T `rr (hl)`) — the big lever; simplified `Ch=g^(e&(f^g))` / `Maj=(a&b)|(c&(a^b))` (no `CPL`); chained rotations (load once, snapshot each term); in-place W schedule; `add4` on LSB pointers; dead subroutines removed. ABI byte-preserved (`sha256_init`/`update`/`final` + `SHA_H`/`SHA_BITLEN`/`SHA_FILL`/`SHA_BLOCK`). **729,795 → 418,843 T/block (−42.6%, 1.74×)** — the **fastest verified Z80 SHA-256** (vs timewave0/ZARC ~480–485k; oisee's "45k" is fabricated AI code that doesn't assemble/run/hash). Byte-exact: NIST KAT (incl. the million-'a' long vector) + the real `start.elf` (2.98 MB) → `dd9b4204…ec84` + full `ci-netboot-z80`; TLS composite fits (`tls_client_end` &F7BD < &FB00, 835 B). The 8× unroll/circular-renaming (~9% more) was correct-in-isolation but reverted on a pyz80 `EQU FOR` rewind fragility — follow-up under i102m. | DONE — ✅ PR #365 (sha256 −42.6%, 729.8K→418.8K T/block) | `src/netboot/sha256.asm`; i102m, i100, i101; q21 |
| **i102o** | i102 **sha256 8× circular-renaming unroll — increment 2**. Unrolls the 64 rounds into an 8-phase group looped 8×: with circular renaming a..h never physically move, so the per-round 28-byte `lddr` shuffle (~37k T/block of data movement) is gone — each phase hard-codes which `wv_` slot is a..h. **418,843 → 377,371 T/block (−9.9%)**; cumulative sha256 729,795→377,371 (−48.3%). Generated **flat** (ordinary mnemonics, no pyz80 `EQU FOR`/macros — sidesteps the `EQU FOR`×macro rewind bug that reverted the first attempt) by `tools/sha256-unroll-gen/gen_sha256_unrolled.py`; the committed inline block is **drift-guarded byte-for-byte** by `regen_guard_test.go` (in `ci-netboot-z80`). **Gated** behind `if defined(NETBOOT_TLS_CLIENT)` — the TLS composite keeps the smaller rolled loop (`tls_client_end` &F7BD < &FB00 unchanged); only the standalone/firmware build unrolls (~2.4 KB). Pre-req: `sha_ch`/`sha_maj` take caller-set source pointers (both paths). Byte-exact: NIST KAT + million-'a' + the real `start.elf` (2.98 MB) → `dd9b4204…ec84` + full `ci-netboot-z80`; `TestSHA256PerBlockCompress` pins ≤418,843. | DONE — ✅ PR #367 (sha256 −9.9%, 418.8K→377.4K T/block) | `src/netboot/sha256.asm`; `tools/sha256-unroll-gen/`; i102m, i102n, i100, i101 |
| **i102p** | i102 **sha256 inline Ch/Maj — increment 3**. In the unrolled (firmware/standalone) round path the operand slots for `Ch(e,f,g)` and `Maj(a,b,c)` are compile-time constants, so the 8 generated phases now inline both instead of calling the shared `sha_ch`/`sha_maj` subroutines. The subroutines take their three source pointers at runtime and so pay a **19 T `(ix+d)`/`(iy+d)` indexed access per operand byte** plus `call`/`djnz` overhead; inline, each operand loads with a plain `ld a,(nnnn)` and the boolean is done in A with H,L,D as scratch (`Ch` 76 T/byte vs ~135; `Maj` 92 T/byte vs ~141). **377,371 → 346,267 T/block (−8.2%)**; cumulative sha256 729,795→346,267 (−52.6%). Emitted by `tools/sha256-unroll-gen/gen_sha256_unrolled.py` (`ch_inline`/`maj_inline`), drift-guarded byte-for-byte by `regen_guard_test.go`. The shared subroutines are untouched — the rolled `NETBOOT_TLS_CLIENT` composite still calls them, so its binary is byte-identical (built green, budget unchanged). Byte-exact: full NIST KAT + the million-'a' long vector (15,625 blocks through the unrolled compress) + full `ci-netboot-z80`; `TestSHA256PerBlockCompress` now pins ≤346,267. | DONE — ✅ PR #369 (sha256 −8.2%, 377.4K→346.3K T/block) | `src/netboot/sha256.asm`; `tools/sha256-unroll-gen/`; i102m, i102n, i102o, i100, i101 |
| **i102q** | i102 sha256 increment 4 — **alternating `sha_tmpa` forward/backward walk** in the σ/Σ XOR passes. `sha_bigsigma0`/`sha_bigsigma1`/`sha_sigma0`/`sha_sigma1` interleave one `st_bcde_tmpa` + 1–2 `xor_bcde_tmpa` passes over the 4-byte `sha_tmpa`, each of which reloaded `ld hl, sha_tmpa` (10 T). Since the intervening rotate macros preserve HL and XOR is per-byte order-independent, each pass after the first can chain from where the previous left HL: new `xor_bcde_tmpa_bwd` (from `sha_tmpa+3`) / `xor_bcde_tmpa_fwd` (from `sha_tmpa`) macros drop the reload. Saves 2 reloads each in the pure-rotate Σ functions (×64 rounds) + 1 each in the σ functions (×48 extend words) = **−3520 T/block: 346,267 → 342,747 (−1.0%)**. NIST KAT byte-exact (the guardrail); the change is confined to the hand-written σ/Σ helpers, so the 8×-unroll generator + its regen guard are untouched. Per-block floor in `tstates_baseline_test.go` lowered to 342,747. | DONE — ✅ PR #375 | `src/netboot/sha256.asm` (σ/Σ helpers + the `xor_bcde_tmpa_{bwd,fwd}` macros); `tools/netboot-oracle/z80/tstates_baseline_test.go`; i102m (umbrella), i102p |
| **i102r** | i102 sha256 increment 5 — **register-accumulator round** (the last sizeable architectural lever). The unrolled round previously summed `T1 = h + S1(e) + Ch + K[t] + W[t]` and `T2 = S0(a) + Maj` memory-to-memory: each of the seven 32-bit term adds was a ~124 T carry chain that wrote its 4-byte result back to RAM only for the next add to re-read it. Now T1 then T2 are accumulated in the register quad **B,C,D,E** (B=MSB..E=LSB, big-endian) — each term folds in with a `BCDE += memory` carry chain (~92 T, no write-back), so the four intermediate stores of the T1 chain + both of the T2 chain are gone; only the final `new e`/`new a` words touch memory. The shared sigma subroutines own B,C,D,E to rotate, so each sigma is computed to `sha_tmpa` first and the accumulator loaded after it returns; `Ch` rewritten to use A,H,L only (so it runs while T1 sits in B,C,D,E), `Maj` parks in new `sha_majs` while S0 holds `sha_tmpa`. Emitted by `gen_sha256_unrolled.py` (rewritten `phase_real` + `bcde_add`/`mem_add_bcde`/`ld_bcde_from`/`st_bcde` helpers), drift-guarded byte-for-byte by `regen_guard_test.go`. **342,747 → 315,355 T/block (−8.0%)**; cumulative sha256 729,795→315,355 (**−56.8%**). Bonus: the more compact sequence also **shrinks** the emitted block, so the firmware-fetch bootable (`http_main`, unrolled path) drops from end-`&ECED` to `&EB0F` (−478 B, 5361 B free under `&10000`) — faster *and* smaller. Composite untouched (rolled path; +4 B `sha_majs` data only: `tls_client_end` &F7AF < &FB00, 849 B clearance). Byte-exact: full NIST KAT + chunked vectors + full `ci-netboot-z80`; floor pinned ≤315,355. | DONE — ✅ PR #377 | `src/netboot/sha256.asm`; `tools/sha256-unroll-gen/`; `tools/netboot-oracle/z80/tstates_baseline_test.go`; i102m (umbrella), i102q |
| **i127** | **netboot client: transmit the ARP only once the ENC PHY link is up** (root-caused 2026-06-18 via the i124 boot-path emulation). `client_main` broadcast its proactive ARP via `client_first` immediately after `drv_init`, but `drv_init` never waited for PHY link-up; on real hardware the first ARP was clocked into the ENC before the 10BASE-T link was up, the PHY silently dropped it, and the client livelocked awaiting a reply that couldn't come (the flickering-border, silent-wire symptom). `smoke_main` was unaffected (reactive — first TX is a reply, by which time the link is up). **Fix (a):** `drv_wait_link` (`src/netboot/enc_link.asm`) polls PHSTAT2.LSTAT over the MII until the link is up, called in `client_main` between `drv_init` and `client_first`. Built + emulation-code-verified PR #424; **HARDWARE-CONFIRMED 2026-06-19** — rec 13 ran the full network path (border flashing) and failed only at the later B-DOS write-out (i119), versus the old dead-silent client. The deferred emulator model + silent-wire reproduction + regression test (the i124 "remaining" piece) landed as **i131** (this PR). **Fix (b)** — ARP-retransmit-on-timeout in `cl_fetch_loop` — was the "and/or" alternative and is NOT implemented: (a) alone is hardware-confirmed sufficient, so no test is weakened by its absence (a robustness nice-to-have, not a gap). `http_main` carries the same latent bug; applying `drv_wait_link` there is the separate open item i128. | DONE — ✅ fix (a) PR #424, hardware-confirmed rec 13; emulator model + reproduction PR #427 (i131) | `src/netboot/enc_link.asm` (`drv_wait_link`/`rd_phy_reg`); `src/netboot/netboot_client.asm` (`client_main`); `tools/netboot-oracle/z80/enc28j60.go`; i124, i82, i119, i128, i131 |
| **i131** | **Feed the hardware-confirmed PHY-link-up behaviour back into the emulator (the i127 rule-7 loop).** With the i127 fix hardware-confirmed (rec 13 ran the full network path, failing only at the i119 write-out vs the old dead-silent client) — the deliberate emulation-first gate (confirm a not-yet-modelled failure on hardware, *then* model it) — the silent wire is now modelled: `tools/netboot-oracle/z80/enc28j60.go` `doTransmit` **drops a frame transmitted while the PHY link is down** (still signalling TXIF/TXRTS so the driver believes it succeeded — the datasheet behaviour that hides the loss), atop the already-modelled PHSTAT2.LSTAT down-then-up read (#424). Regression tests: `TestDrvWriteSilentWireWhileLinkDown` (low-level — `drv_write` while link down egresses nothing, then egresses once up; the un-fixed proactive-transmitter reproduction) + `TestClientBootRecoversFromLinkDownStart` (integration — the fixed `client_main` with the link starting down still egresses exactly its one ARP because `drv_wait_link` blocks; self-calibrating via the new `FirstTXOps` so removing the fix → 0 frames → red, proven by temporarily disabling the call). So the whole transmit-before-link-up class is caught in emulation forever; link-up *timing* stays hardware-gated (CLAUDE.md §5). | DONE — ✅ PR #427 | `tools/netboot-oracle/z80/enc28j60.go` (`doTransmit` drop + `FirstTXOps`); `tools/netboot-oracle/z80/{enc28j60_test,netboot_boot_test}.go`; `tools/netboot-oracle/z80/README.md`; i127, i124, i126 |
| **i128** | Apply `drv_wait_link` to `http_main` (the 2nd proactive netboot transmitter) — the one-line mirror of the hardware-confirmed i127 client fix: wait for PHY link after `drv_init` before `prov_first`'s ARP, with a yellow-border (6) fail path. Emulation regression `http_main_boot_test.go` (`TestHTTPMainBootReachesFirstTX` + `TestHTTPMainRecoversFromLinkDownStart`, flat-loaded) mirrors the client's; the silent-wire model (i131) makes removing the fix go red. | DONE — PR #432 | `src/netboot/http_main.asm`; `src/netboot/enc_link.asm`; `tools/netboot-oracle/z80/http_main_boot_test.go`; i127, i70, i82 |
| **i136** | **Autonomous-loop monitor: the resume nudge was stuffed into `/context`'s redraw and dropped** (Pete hit it 2026-06-19 — only `/context` arrived after a merged item; the agent sat idle until he woke it by hand). The `TASK_DONE` branch fired `stuff "$RESUME_NUDGE"` immediately after submitting `/context`, with no settle. `/context` is a *local* command that redraws the whole TUI; nudge keystrokes stuffed during that redraw are lost, so the nudge never became a turn — the same ~1h-stall class as the earlier "/context alone never resumed" bug, but caused by the nudge racing the render rather than being absent. Fix: sleep `ALOOP_CONTEXT_SETTLE` (default 5s) between the `/context` submit and the nudge stuff — the `/context` analogue of `CLEAR_SETTLE` on the wound-down path. (The 1s `SUBMIT_SETTLE` inside `submit()` was too short to outlast the render.) | DONE — ✅ PR #428 | `tools/autonomous-loop/monitor.sh` (`CONTEXT_SETTLE`); `tools/autonomous-loop/README.md`; q14 (loop still maturing) |
| **i134** | **Harness SD/B-DOS write-out model — run the client's RST-8 / HRECORD / HSAVE persistence in emulation (the i124/i126 SD-store slice; prerequisite for emulation-testing i119).** The boot-wrapper harness modelled the ENC + EEPROM but not the B-DOS hooks (`bdos_select_record`/`bdos_save_hook`, behind `ifndef NETBOOT_HOSTTEST`), so the client write-out was carved out of emulation and shipped to hardware uncaught (CLAUDE.md rule 7). Added a generic RST-vector hook to the harness (`harness.go` `rstHandlers` — intercepts PC at a registered RST target, pops the pushed return address, runs a handler, resumes past the inline operand) and `bdos_store.go` (`BDOSStore` + `Machine.AttachBDOS`) modelling HRECORD (record select) + HSAVE (capture+decode the 48-byte UIFA at IX). `bdos_store_test.go`: a mechanism test (`bdos_select_record`/`bdos_save_hook` called directly → record + UIFA captured) + `TestClientBootWriteOutCaptured` (the real bootable `client_main` driven through a minimal TFTP transfer to its write-out, asserting the captured save). The end-to-end test **reproduces the i119 record-selection bug in emulation** (client_main selects record 0 = floppy) and surfaced a new finding (filename >10 chars truncates in the SAM UIFA name field — noted on i119). Models the digital dispatch, not a real persist (CLAUDE.md §5). | DONE — ✅ PR #429 | `tools/netboot-oracle/z80/bdos_store.go`, `bdos_store_test.go`, `harness.go` (`rstHandlers`); `tools/netboot-oracle/z80/README.md`; `src/netboot/bdos_seam.asm`; i119, i124, i126 |
| **i132** | **Run trinload itself in the emulation harness — validate the push→run→return cycle.** Load `trinload.bin` (`org &6000`, vendored at `src/netboot/trinload.asm`) in the koron-z80 harness with the i124 ENC + EEPROM emulation; inject the trinload UDP protocol (`?` → `!` reply; `@`+page+offset+data → LDIR into RAM at correct offset; `X`+page+addr → pushed program executes + RETs back to trinload `start`). Confirms control returns to trinload after the pushed program RETs (`try_exec` does `ld hl,start; push hl` before `jp (hl)`). De-risks every future trinload push. Shipped: vendored `src/netboot/trinload.asm` (reuses the vendored encdrv/eeprom), `make netboot-trinload`, `trinload_test.go` (`TestTrinloadPushRunReturn` + `TestTrinloadDataWriteToOffset`), `LoadAt`/`StopPC`/`StopPCSkip` in `harness.go`, HMPR snoop in `enc28j60.go`. | DONE — PR #431 | `tools/netboot-oracle/z80/trinload_test.go`; `src/netboot/trinload.asm`; i124, i126, i129, i133 |
| **i125** | **Section-C fit gate for every bootable netboot program (build + CI).** A bootable netboot program loads at `&8000`; `&C000-&FFFF` is ROM1 at boot, so code/data above the budget is never written to RAM and the program crashes on its first call there (the i119 over-size class that wasted a hardware session). Generalised the down-payment client check to **all** `*_boot.bin` targets via one shared script `tools/netboot-boot-fit-check.sh` (single home for the budget logic, replacing the two divergent inline checks): smoke/serve/server/client gated to 16384 (section C), http to 32768 (the §13.1 section-D overlay). Wired into CI by adding `netboot-server-boot` + `netboot-serve-boot` to `netboot-z80-routines` (the `ci-netboot-z80` build) so an over-size bootable image fails CI, not hardware. | DONE — PR #433 | `tools/netboot-boot-fit-check.sh`; `Makefile` (the five `*_boot.bin` rules + `netboot-z80-routines`); `docs/specs/phase3-delivery-design.md` §13.1; i119, i124 |
